---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import SatelliteSlider from "../components/SatelliteSlider.svelte";
import TeacherVoice from "../components/TeacherVoice.astro";
import EvidenceHunt from "../components/EvidenceHunt.svelte";
import ExitTicket from "../components/ExitTicket.svelte";
// Image Imports
import nightImg from "../assets/korea-night.jpg";
import mapImg from "../assets/korea-map.jpg";

const title = "The Unfinished War";
---

<Layout title={title}>
  <main class="max-w-5xl mx-auto py-20 px-6">
    <header class="mb-20 text-center">
      <h1 class="text-5xl font-black text-slate-900 mb-4">{title}</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-slate-600 max-w-2xl mx-auto italic">
        Why is one half of the peninsula glowing while the other is pitch black?
      </p>
    </header>

    <section
      id="section-hook"
      class="mb-64 scroll-mt-24 relative"
      data-insight="If you were looking at this from space, where would you think the border is? Why is one half glowing and the other almost pitch black?"
    >
      <div class="mb-8">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">
          The Line of Light
        </h2>
        <p class="text-slate-600">
          Use the slider to compare the 1953 armistice line with modern
          satellite imagery.
        </p>
      </div>

      <SatelliteSlider
        client:load
        nightImage={nightImg.src}
        mapImage={mapImg.src}
      />

      <div
        class="mt-16 flex flex-col items-center justify-center text-slate-400 animate-bounce"
      >
        <span
          class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] mb-2 text-slate-500"
        >
          Continue Investigation
        </span>
        <svg
          xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
          class="h-6 w-6 text-blue-500"
          fill="none"
          viewBox="0 0 24 24"
          stroke="currentColor"
          stroke-width="2"
        >
          <path
            stroke-linecap="round"
            stroke-linejoin="round"
            d="M19 14l-7 7m0 0l-7-7m7 7V3"></path>
        </svg>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="section-intro" class="mb-32 scroll-mt-24">
      <!-- Lesson Introduction & Hook -->
      <div class="my-10">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 mb-6">
          The Unfinished War: Korea and the Cold War's Lasting Legacy
        </h2>

        <p class="text-lg text-slate-800 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          If you look at the Korean Peninsula from space at night, you will see
          a striking divide: one half glows with the bright lights of modern,
          bustling cities, while the other is almost entirely pitch black. If you didn't have a map, you might think the glowing half was an
          island.
        </p>

        <p class="text-lg text-slate-800 leading-relaxed mb-8">
          What caused this massive difference? That "Line of Light" is the
          direct result of a geopolitical decision made in 1953. In
          this lesson, we will explore the course and consequences of the Korean
          War. We'll investigate how a localized conflict became the
          first major battleground of the Cold War, and why the decisions made
          by global superpowers over seventy years ago still shape the politics,
          economics, and daily lives of millions of people today.
        </p>

        <!-- Learning Objective Callout -->
        <div
          class="p-5 bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-600 rounded-r-lg shadow-sm"
        >
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
            <span class="text-blue-600 text-lg" aria-hidden="true">🎯</span>
            <h4 class="font-bold text-blue-900 text-lg m-0">
              Mission Objective
            </h4>
          </div>
          <p class="text-blue-800 leading-relaxed m-0">
            By the end of this module, you will be able to explain how the
            Korean War’s armistice ending led to at least two present-day
            consequences, using evidence from historical maps and text.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section
      id="section-leadup"
      class="mb-32 scroll-mt-24"
      data-insight="Here I provide information about the leadup to the Korean War, such as the Japanese occupation"
    >
      <!-- Pre-War Context: Japanese Annexation -->
      <div
        class="my-8 p-6 bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-l-blue-600 shadow-sm"
      >
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">
          Before the Division: The Japanese Empire (1910–1945)
        </h3>
        <p class="text-slate-800 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          To understand why Korea was divided in the first place, we have to
          look at the decades just before the Cold War. Korea was absorbed into
          the Empire of Japan in 1910. For 35 years, the Korean people endured
          harsh colonial rule that attempted to erase their culture and heavily
          extracted their resources.
        </p>
        <p class="text-slate-800 leading-relaxed">
          Korea was freed after Japan’s defeat in WWII in 1945. However, this
          sudden liberation created a massive power vacuum. The victorious
          Allied powers (specifically the US and Soviet Union) divided the
          peninsula at the 38th parallel to oversee the surrender of Japanese
          troops. What was meant to be a temporary administrative line quickly
          became one of the most dangerous borders in the world.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section
      id="section-origins"
      class="mb-32 scroll-mt-24"
      data-insight="I emphasize that the Koreans didn't choose this division. After 35 years of Japanese occupation, their peninsula was split along the 38th parallel by two external superpowers."
    >
      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-12 items-center">
        <div>
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">
            A Peninsula Divided
          </h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-6">
            Following Japan's defeat in World War II in 1945, the Korean
            peninsula was freed from 35 years of colonial rule. However,
            liberation quickly turned into division. The United States and the
            Soviet Union divided the territory at the 38th Parallel.
          </p>
          <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-500 p-6 rounded-r-xl">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-blue-900 mb-2">The Cold War Proxy</h3>
            <p class="text-blue-800 text-sm">
              The US became involved under the policy of <strong
                >Containment</strong
              >—the Truman Doctrine's goal to stop the global spread of
              communism.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-4">
          <div
            class="bg-white p-6 rounded-3xl shadow-sm border border-slate-200 text-center"
          >
            <div
              class="aspect-square bg-slate-100 rounded-full mb-4 flex items-center justify-center overflow-hidden border-4 border-white shadow-inner"
            >
              <span class="text-slate-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase"
                >Rhee</span
              >
            </div>
            <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900">Syngman Rhee</h4>
            <p
              class="text-[10px] text-slate-500 uppercase tracking-widest mt-1"
            >
              South Korea
            </p>
            <p class="text-xs text-blue-600 font-medium mt-3">
              Backed by UN & US
            </p>
          </div>
          <div
            class="bg-white p-6 rounded-3xl shadow-sm border border-slate-200 text-center"
          >
            <div
              class="aspect-square bg-slate-100 rounded-full mb-4 flex items-center justify-center overflow-hidden border-4 border-white shadow-inner"
            >
              <span class="text-slate-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase">Kim</span
              >
            </div>
            <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900">Kim Il Sung</h4>
            <p
              class="text-[10px] text-slate-500 uppercase tracking-widest mt-1"
            >
              North Korea
            </p>
            <p class="text-xs text-red-600 font-medium mt-3">
              Backed by China & USSR
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="section-leaders" class="mb-32 scroll-mt-24">
      <!-- The Leaders: Competing Visions for Korea -->
      <div class="my-8">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">
          The Leaders: Competing Visions for Korea
        </h3>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
          <!-- Syngman Rhee Profile -->
          <div
            class="p-6 bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-lg border-t-4 border-t-blue-600 shadow-sm flex flex-col items-center text-center"
          >
            <div
              class="w-20 h-20 bg-slate-200 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center mb-4 border-2 border-white shadow-sm"
            >
              <span class="text-3xl" aria-hidden="true">🇰🇷</span>
            </div>
            <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-blue-800 mb-1">Syngman Rhee</h4>
            <p
              class="text-sm font-semibold text-slate-500 uppercase tracking-wider mb-3"
            >
              President of South Korea
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
              Led the US-backed, non-communist government in the South. After the 1945 division at the 38th parallel, his government
              claimed to be the legitimate ruler of the entire peninsula.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Kim Il Sung Profile -->
          <div
            class="p-6 bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-lg border-t-4 border-t-red-600 shadow-sm flex flex-col items-center text-center"
          >
            <div
              class="w-20 h-20 bg-slate-200 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center mb-4 border-2 border-white shadow-sm"
            >
              <span class="text-3xl" aria-hidden="true">🇰🇵</span>
            </div>
            <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-800 mb-1">Kim Il Sung</h4>
            <p
              class="text-sm font-semibold text-slate-500 uppercase tracking-wider mb-3"
            >
              President of North Korea
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
              Led the Soviet-backed communist regime in the North.
              Like his counterpart in the South, his government also claimed
              legitimate authority over all of Korea, ultimately initiating the
              invasion on June 25, 1950[cite: 2, 3, 4].
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section
      id="section-allies"
      class="mb-32 scroll-mt-24"
      data-insight="Why did the US even get involved? Let's look at a policy that shaped the entire Cold War era."
    >
      <!-- Geopolitical Alliances Section -->
      <div class="my-8">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">
          The Global Alliances: A Divided Peninsula
        </h3>
        <p class="text-slate-800 leading-relaxed mb-6">
          The conflict on the Korean Peninsula quickly escalated from a
          localized civil war into a major proxy conflict of the Cold War era.
          Both sides relied heavily on powerful global allies to sustain their
          war effort.
        </p>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
          <!-- South Korea and Allies -->
          <div
            class="p-6 bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-lg border-t-4 border-t-blue-600 shadow-sm"
          >
            <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-blue-800 mb-2">
              Defending South Korea
            </h4>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-slate-800">
              <li>
                <strong>United Nations Coalition:</strong> Backed by a historic UN
                resolution, a multi-nation coalition stepped in to support the South.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>United States Support:</strong> The vast majority of the UN
                forces were composed of American troops sent to enforce containment
                policy.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>International Aid:</strong> Major military and logistical
                backing was also provided by the UK, Australia, Canada, and many other
                nations.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <!-- North Korea and Allies -->
          <div
            class="p-6 bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-lg border-t-4 border-t-red-600 shadow-sm"
          >
            <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-800 mb-2">
              Supporting North Korea
            </h4>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-slate-800">
              <li>
                <strong>People's Republic of China:</strong> Provided massive military
                intervention, sending hundreds of thousands of combat troops directly
                across the border to fight.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>The Soviet Union:</strong> Avoided direct, official troop
                deployment but heavily supplied the North with crucial military equipment,
                heavy weaponry, and aircraft.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="section-containment" class="mb-32 scroll-mt-24">
      <!-- Ideological Framework: Containment Policy -->
      <div
        class="my-8 p-6 bg-amber-50/60 border border-amber-200 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-l-amber-500 shadow-sm"
      >
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2">
          <span class="text-amber-600 text-xl font-bold" aria-hidden="true"
            >💡</span
          >
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 m-0">
            Cold War Framework: What is Containment?
          </h3>
        </div>

        <p class="text-slate-800 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          To understand why the United States sent hundreds of thousands of
          troops to fight on a small peninsula thousands of miles away, you have
          to understand the global US policy known as <strong
            >Containment</strong
          >.
        </p>

        <blockquote
          class="pl-4 my-4 border-l-2 border-slate-300 italic text-slate-700"
        >
          "Containment was the foundational Cold War foreign policy of the
          United States, designed to stop the spread of communism beyond the
          countries where it already existed."
        </blockquote>

        <p class="text-slate-800 leading-relaxed">
          Following World War II, American leaders believed that the Soviet
          Union and its allies would continuously attempt to expand their
          spheres of political influence[cite: 4]. Rather than launching a
          direct, catastrophic war to eliminate communism where it was already
          established, the US chose to draw a hard line in the sand[cite: 4].
          The Korean War became the very first major, physical test case where
          this policy was put into action[cite: 4].
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section
      id="section-timeline"
      class="mb-32 scroll-mt-24"
      data-insight="I use this breakdown to show students how wildly momentum swung in just one year. From being pinned at Pusan, to reaching the Chinese border, and being pushed back again—it shatters the misconception that wars are slow, predictable marches."
    >
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-12 text-center">
        The Course of the War
      </h2>
      <div
        class="max-w-2xl mx-auto relative border-l-2 border-slate-200 ml-4 md:mx-auto"
      >
        <div class="mb-12 ml-10 relative">
          <div
            class="absolute -left-[49px] top-1 w-4 h-4 rounded-full bg-red-500 ring-4 ring-white"
          >
          </div>
          <span class="text-sm font-bold text-red-500 uppercase tracking-widest"
            >Summer 1950</span
          >
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-2">
            The Invasion & Pusan Perimeter
          </h3>
          <p class="text-slate-600 mt-3 leading-relaxed">
            North Korean forces launch a massive surprise invasion, rapidly
            overwhelming the South. US and UN forces are pushed all the way to
            the southeastern tip of the peninsula, desperately holding a small
            defensive line known as the Pusan Perimeter.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mb-12 ml-10 relative">
          <div
            class="absolute -left-[49px] top-1 w-4 h-4 rounded-full bg-blue-500 ring-4 ring-white"
          >
          </div>
          <span
            class="text-sm font-bold text-blue-500 uppercase tracking-widest"
            >September 1950</span
          >
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-2">
            The Incheon Landing
          </h3>
          <p class="text-slate-600 mt-3 leading-relaxed">
            General Douglas MacArthur executes a daring amphibious assault
            behind enemy lines at Incheon. The brilliant maneuver cuts off North
            Korean supply lines, allowing UN forces to break out of Pusan,
            recapture Seoul, and push deep into North Korea toward the Yalu
            River.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mb-12 ml-10 relative">
          <div
            class="absolute -left-[49px] top-1 w-4 h-4 rounded-full bg-orange-600 ring-4 ring-white"
          >
          </div>
          <span
            class="text-sm font-bold text-orange-600 uppercase tracking-widest"
            >Winter 1950</span
          >
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-2">
            Chinese Entry & Chosin Reservoir
          </h3>
          <p class="text-slate-600 mt-3 leading-relaxed">
            Fearing a US presence on its border, China enters the war with a
            massive, stealthy offensive. In sub-zero temperatures at the Chosin
            Reservoir, surrounded UN forces must fight a brutal, fighting
            retreat to the coast to avoid annihilation.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mb-12 ml-10 relative">
          <div
            class="absolute -left-[49px] top-1 w-4 h-4 rounded-full bg-red-600 ring-4 ring-white"
          >
          </div>
          <span class="text-sm font-bold text-red-600 uppercase tracking-widest"
            >Early 1951</span
          >
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-2">
            The Communist Counterattack
          </h3>
          <p class="text-slate-600 mt-3 leading-relaxed">
            The combined Chinese and North Korean forces push the UN army back
            across the 38th parallel. Seoul falls to communist forces once again
            in a devastating setback for the UN coalition.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mb-12 ml-10 relative">
          <div
            class="absolute -left-[49px] top-1 w-4 h-4 rounded-full bg-slate-400 ring-4 ring-white"
          >
          </div>
          <span
            class="text-sm font-bold text-slate-500 uppercase tracking-widest"
            >1951 - 1953</span
          >
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-2">
            The Grinding Stalemate
          </h3>
          <p class="text-slate-600 mt-3 leading-relaxed">
            UN forces regroup and push the lines back near the original 38th
            parallel. The war of massive movement devolves into brutal trench
            warfare. For two years, soldiers fight and die over small hills
            while peace negotiations continually stall.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="section-video" class="mb-40 scroll-mt-24">
      <!-- The Battle for Seoul: Back-and-Forth Fighting -->
      <div class="my-10">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">
          The Battle for Seoul: A Peninsula in Motion
        </h3>

        <p class="text-slate-800 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          While battle lines eventually stabilized, the first year of the Korean
          War was defined by extreme, volatile shifts in territory[cite: 3, 4].
          No city experienced this devastating volatility more than the capital, <strong
            >Seoul</strong
          >, which changed hands an incredible <strong>four times</strong> during
          the first twelve months of active combat[cite: 3].
        </p>

        <p class="text-slate-800 leading-relaxed mb-6">
          First captured during the initial North Korean invasion in June 1950,
          the city was liberated by UN forces following the daring Inchon
          landing in September[cite: 3, 4]. However, when China entered the war
          later that winter, UN forces were pushed back, and Seoul fell a third
          time in January 1951, before being permanently recaptured by the UN
          coalition that March[cite: 3].
        </p>

        <!-- Video Map Animation Component -->
        <div
          class="p-6 bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-lg shadow-sm"
        >
          <div class="flex flex-col md:flex-row md:items-center gap-6">
            <div class="flex-1">
              <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">
                Visualizing the Conflict: Dynamic Map Animation
              </h4>
              <p class="text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4">
                To truly understand how rapidly the front lines moved across the
                peninsula before settling into a brutal stalemate, take a look
                at this concise, one-minute map animation detailing the shifting
                borders of the war.
              </p>
              <a
                href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aNrd0Tv4pEY"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
                class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-2 bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white font-semibold text-sm rounded-md transition shadow-sm"
              >
                <span>▶</span> Watch the 1-Minute Map Animation
              </a>
            </div>

            <!-- Video Placeholder Frame to maintain clean visual balance -->
            <div
              class="w-full md:w-64 bg-slate-200 border border-slate-300 rounded aspect-video flex flex-col items-center justify-center p-4 text-center"
            >
              <span class="text-slate-400 text-3xl mb-1" aria-hidden="true"
                >🗺️</span
              >
              <span
                class="text-slate-600 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider"
                >Territorial Shifts</span
              >
              <span class="text-slate-500 text-[11px] mt-1"
                >1950–1953 Animation</span
              >
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section
      id="section-armistice"
      class="mb-40 scroll-mt-24"
      data-insight="This is the most crucial vocabulary distinction of the entire unit. An armistice is a pause button, not a stop button. That distinction is the only way to understand why the DMZ exists today."
    >
      <div
        class="bg-slate-900 text-white rounded-3xl p-10 md:p-16 text-center shadow-2xl relative overflow-hidden border-4 border-slate-800"
      >
        <div
          class="absolute top-0 left-0 w-full h-2 bg-gradient-to-r from-blue-500 via-white to-red-500"
        >
        </div>

        <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-black mb-6 tracking-tight">
          July 27, 1953:<br />The Armistice
        </h2>
        <p
          class="text-xl text-slate-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed mb-8"
        >
          An agreement was signed to end the fighting. But it was <strong
            class="text-white border-b-2 border-red-500 pb-1"
            >NOT a peace treaty</strong
          >. Both governments still consider themselves the legitimate ruler of
          all Korea.
        </p>

        <div
          class="inline-flex items-center gap-4 bg-white/10 px-6 py-3 rounded-full border border-white/20 shadow-inner"
        >
          <span class="w-3 h-3 rounded-full bg-red-500 animate-pulse"></span>
          <span class="font-medium tracking-wide uppercase text-sm"
            >The war technically continues today</span
          >
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section
      id="section-legacy"
      class="mb-32 scroll-mt-24"
      data-insight="I want students to see how a single historical pivot point created two entirely distinct modern realities. Connecting the 1953 ceasefire to the tech, pop culture, and geopolitics they see on their phones today makes the history feel immediate and real."
    >
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4 text-center">
        One Peninsula, Two Worlds
      </h2>
      <p class="text-slate-600 text-center max-w-xl mx-auto mb-16">
        The signing of the armistice froze the conflict lines but set off two
        radically different trajectories for the people living on either side.
      </p>

      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 mb-12">
        <div
          class="bg-gradient-to-b from-blue-50/50 to-white p-8 rounded-3xl border border-slate-200 shadow-sm"
        >
          <div
            class="inline-flex items-center justify-center w-12 h-12 bg-blue-500 text-white font-bold rounded-2xl mb-6 shadow-md shadow-blue-500/20"
          >
            S
          </div>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">
            The Miracle on the Han River
          </h3>
          <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-6">
            Though left devastated and deeply impoverished after the war, South
            Korea underwent a massive economic transformation. Initially ruled
            by military dictators, the public successfully demanded free
            democratic elections in 1987.
          </p>
          <ul class="space-y-3 border-t border-slate-100 pt-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-3 text-sm text-slate-600">
              <span class="text-blue-500 font-bold">✓</span>
              <span
                ><strong>Global Tech Giant:</strong> Home to leading brands like Samsung
                and LG.</span
              >
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-3 text-sm text-slate-600">
              <span class="text-blue-500 font-bold">✓</span>
              <span
                ><strong>Cultural Exporter:</strong> Massive international influence
                driven by K-pop and media.</span
              >
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-3 text-sm text-slate-600">
              <span class="text-blue-500 font-bold">✓</span>
              <span
                ><strong>Democracy:</strong> A highly prosperous, free society built
                from the ground up.</span
              >
